{"id": "cd1a4a1d-9254-4218-a0ff-ae6e36f58161", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T01:13:09.966989", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "7fa8d30f-39e3-4d2b-a0d9-6e2628ad5cf5", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-a9bfab96-4644-489b-99c6-01647f7015b4", "tool_call": {"id": "call-a9bfab96-4644-489b-99c6-01647f7015b4", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"summary":"Submit plan for torsion-dynamics recovery test","stage":"plan","answer":"**Test question, refined.** Given a fine-cadence all-atom ground-truth trajectory (Δt ≈ fs–ps) and a subsampled version at MDGEN's training stride, can MDGEN regenerate the *fast* torsion oscillations (φ, ψ, χ) that the subsampling destroyed?\n\n**Approach.**\n- For each system: take a fine ground-truth trajectory at original cadence, construct the subsampled version at the stride S that MDGEN was trained for, feed subsampled frames as MDGEN's context, and let MDGEN emit the intermediate frames — then re-extract torsions at the original cadence.\n- Compare in torsion space (not cartesian), since the question is explicitly about oscillations in torsion angles.\n\n**Metric battery (ranked).**\n1. Welch PSD of each torsion; integrated power in the high-frequency band lost under subsampling — primary metric.\n2. Autocorrelation time τ_c per torsion; relative error to ground truth.\n3. 1D F(φ), F(ψ) and 2D Ramachandran via JSD to ground truth.\n4. MSM/HMM implied timescales on the discretized torsion (lag-time sweep).\n5. Hilbert-envelope amplitude & zero-crossing period of each oscillation cycle.\n6. RMSD in cartesian and torsion displacement — secondary, sanity only.\n\n**Consequential choices needing your decision.**\n- *System:* primary = alanine dipeptide (cleanest ϕ/ψ dynamics, short, easy ground truth). Secondary = chignolin (in-distribution for MDGEN).\n- *Ground truth:* a public alanine-dipeptide fine-cadence trajectory (mdtraj/tutorial datasets) preferred over generating fresh.\n- *Stride sweep:* S ∈ {1 ns, 10 ns, 100 ns}, fixed generated length at MDGEN's maximum, to expose the stride–accuracy curve.\n- *Controls (required):* L0 constant-copy, L1 linear-in-atoms, L2 linear-in-torsion, L3 exact-subsample. L1 is the non-negotiable beating baseline.\n\n**Uncertainties to resolve before Stage=result.**\n- Whether the question is \"MDGEN recovers the *true* fast dynamics\" (matched against ground truth at fs/ps) or \"MDGEN produces plausible-looking fast dynamics\" (looks correct by marginals). The plan above is the former; please confirm.\n- Statistical power: ≥32 generations per (system, stride, seed) cell for CIs on PSD/τ_c, or whether a smaller n must be acknowledged.\n- Sandbox access: this plan assumes MDGEN repo + weights + mdtraj are available without network. The current env disables network and has no preinstalled deps, so please confirm the experiment will be run in a different harness than this /workspace.","evidence":"Inspected /workspace: only task.json present (single 464-byte file). task.json's track.md states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" Environment notes confirm: bubblewrap sandbox, network disabled, no preinstalled Python deps, no GPU, no datasets. Verified by `ls -la /workspace` and `uname -a`. No executable code exists; no MDGEN repository is present locally; therefore no runtime evidence can or should be produced. The plan is grounded in (a) the published MDGEN training protocol (context spacing ~100 ns, generation of intermediate frames between subsampled MD conditions) as the natural setup to test, and (b) standard torsion-analysis methodology (Welch PSD, autocorrelation, mdtraj-computed φ/ψ/χ, MSM implied timescales) widely used in MD dynamics studies.","assumptions":[{"item":{"item":{"item":{"item":"Topology of generated frames matches the ground-truth topology, allowing identical dihedral definitions — required if the test is moved into a runnable harness.</item>","$text":"Torsion-invariant comparison is the right level of analysis (cartesian RMSD would obfuscate the answer because high-frequency torsion oscillations have negligible cartesian impact).</item>\n"},"$text":"Generated samples are at MDGEN's native per-frame resolution; if MDGEN emits only coarse-grained intermediates (effective Δt ~ several ns), the PSD-integrated-power metric is still defined but interprets the H1 hypothesis as \"MDGEN hallucinates plausible high-frequency torsion oscillations that match ground truth at the corresponding band\" — a stated assumption flagged intentionally.</item>\n"},"$text":"MDGEN was trained at a context spacing on the order of 100 ns (per the MDGEN paper's bridging-frame setup), and the test should match that stride regime to be a fair test of recovery rather than reconstruction of an unseen regime.</item>\n"},"$text":"Ground truth = a fine-cadence all-atom MD trajectory (Δt ≈ fs–10 ps) of a small peptide or protein, with sufficient length that Welch PSD and autocorrelation of ϕ/ψ/χ have resolved values in the fast-oscillation band.</item>\n"}],"uncertainties":[{"item":{"item":{"item":{"item":"Run-time harness: this /workspace has no MDGEN, no Python deps, and network disabled; whether the eventual execution will happen in a different sandbox is unresolved — but does not block the design deliverable.</item>","$text":"Sample size: n≥32 generations per cell is needed for CIs on PSD and τ_c; if compute forces n=10 the design should explicitly acknowledge low statistical power rather than report false significance.</item>\n"},"$text":"Recovery vs. plausibility: whether the question is \"matches ground truth at the corresponding time/cadence\" (strict) or \"torsion marginals/populations are correct\" (looser). Plan is structured for the strict interpretation.</item>\n"},"$text":"Ground-truth trajectory source: public tutorial dataset vs. fresh short simulation vs. relying on whatever MDGEN pre-supplies with the repo.</item>\n"},"$text":"System: alanine dipeptide vs. chignolin vs. both — the user's choice changes whether we're testing in-distribution (chignolin) or out-of-distribution-for-MDGEN-but-best-torsion-benchmark (alanine dipeptide).</item>\n"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-442a722e-b2d6-4d64-8032-fd931b9068ac", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 8}
