{"id": "1c93ae0f-526f-455d-b7a2-83cdd54b6d56", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T01:15:14.281226", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "a74f5a15-943f-41b5-aacc-1474ec77d2f5", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-3a7a7737-e23c-4209-bf24-5f6022d589fd", "tool_call": {"id": "call-3a7a7737-e23c-4209-bf24-5f6022d589fd", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"summary":"Deliver full experimental design document","stage":"result","answer":"# Experimental Design: Does MDGEN recover fast-timestep torsion-angle dynamics lost in subsampled MD?\n\n---\n\n## 0. Reading of the question (locked)\n\n### 0.1 Primary (strict) reading — what \"recovers\" must mean\n\n**H1 (recovery)**: For each backbone / side-chain torsion φ, ψ, χ tracked at the fine ground-truth cadence Δt_truth ≈ fs–ps, the *frequency content of the generated torsion time-series in the spectral band destroyed by subsampling* matches that of the ground truth within its 95% CI. \"Destroyed band\" is formally:\n\n> B_lost(S) = ( f_Nyq(S) , f_Nyq(Δt_truth) ] ,\n> where f_Nyq(Δt) = 1/(2Δt).\n\n**H0 (no recovery)**: Within B_lost, the generated PSD is statistically indistinguishable from a low-pass signal that cannot exceed f_Nyq(S).\n\nThis reading is the *success criterion*. It is a per-dof, per-band, frequentist comparison against the original fs/ps trajectory — not against coarse-grained marginals.\n\n### 0.2 Fallback interpretation A — stride-matched comparison\nIf, after inspection, MDGEN's emitted frames have an effective inter-frame interval Δt_gen ≈ S / N_frames that is close to (or coarser than) Δt_truth's Nyquist bound, then the comparison is reduced to aligning both time-series at the stride resolution and re-running the metric battery. Under this fallback the \"lost band\" is empty and the test reduces to *does MDGEN preserve the *trends* (slowest modes) of torsion dynamics correctly?* — a strictly weaker claim than H1.\n\n### 0.3 Fallback interpretation B — marginals-only\nIf even A is infeasible (e.g., MDGEN emits per-residue snapshots that are not temporally ordered across bridging segments), the comparison is reduced to the *static* distribution: 1D F(φ), F(ψ) and 2D Ramachandran via JSD, plus side-chain rotamer populations. This tests whether MDGEN generates *plausible* torsions, not whether it *recovers* their time-evolution — and should be reported as a strictly weaker negative/positive result.\n\nThe primary / A / B tier is *pre-registered*; whichever tier's reportable statistic is invoked must be so flagged in the final write-up.\n\n---\n\n## 1. System choice\n\n### 1.1 Primary: alanine dipeptide (Ace–Ala–Nme, neutral termini in explicit solvent)\n*Justification*:\n1. **Torsion-only reaction coordinate**: ϕ and ψ are the slow, large-amplitude degrees of freedom; the system's dynamics are *defined* on the 2D Ramachandran surface, so torsion oscillations are the literal quantity of interest rather than a proxy.\n2. **Fast oscillation visibility**: harmonic-like librations around the C7eq / C7ax (or αR / αL under different force fields) basins sit in the 0.1–10 ps regime, well above the Nyquist of any reasonable stride S ≥ 1 ns. The \"lost band\" is therefore wide and easy to characterize.\n3. **Two-state structure**: well-sampled Markovian kinetics on the Ramachandran mean transitions every ~10–100 ps under many force fields, giving an additional plateau for an MSM-based metric.\n4. **Compute footprint**: ~24,000 atoms for a typical 1-nm hydration shell; a 100 ns trajectory at 1 fs stride is small enough to subsample, regenerate, and analyze in seconds.\n5. **OOF for MDGEN by design**: MDGEN was not trained on alanine dipeptide, but this is acceptable — the question is about *physical recovery*, not in-distribution accuracy. If MDGEN *cannot* recover the dihedral oscillations on this cleanest-of-cases system, the answer to the question is negative for any realistic protein in the wild.\n\n### 1.2 Secondary: chignolin (10-residue β-hairpin, CLN025)\n*Justification*:\n1. **In-distribution cross-check**: chignolin is one of the official MDGEN training systems; the experiment there tests whether the model's *own* training-time Fourier content was preserved, which is the strongest-positive case for H1.\n2. **Multiple torsions per residue**: ϕ_i, ψ_i for i = 1…10 plus ~5–8 χ1 side chains gives ≥ 25 tracked degrees of freedom, raising statistical power.\n3. **Slower intrinsic kinetics**: folding transitions are μs-scale, which means the *band of interest* for chignolin dynamics is shifted to lower frequencies; this complements (rather than duplicates) alanine dipeptide.\n4. **Caveat for failure modes**: deeper-residue torsions may have segmentation ambiguity (see §7).\n\nA negative result on chignolin does not refute H1 by itself (in-distribution model on its own training data should at minimum reproduce marginals), but a positive ψ/χ oscillation recovery on chignolin would be a high-impact corroborating signal.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Ground-truth source plan\n\n### 2.1 Source preference order\n1. **Public fine-cadence alanine dipeptide dataset** — preferred. The mdtraj tutorial suite provides an explicit-solvent alanine dipeptide at fs cadence; PyEMMA / PLUMED NGLview tutorials likewise include hydrogen-mass-repartitioned (HMR) short trajectories under AMBER ff99SBildn + TIP3P that are sufficient. Document exact SHA/URL/version of the dataset; checksum on use.\n2. **DESRES / public Anton trajectory of chignolin** at native 1 ns or finer reporting.\n3. **Fallback: a fresh local run** in the execution harness using AMBER ff14SB / OpenFF 2.x + TIP3P, 100 ns at 1 fs stride, HMR if the integrator requires a 4 fs outer step. This is treated as a tertiary backstop — adds compute and reproducibility risk.\n\n### 2.2 Minimum length for Welch PSD convergence in the target band\nWelch's method converges when the *number of independent period-cycles* per segment window is large enough that segment averaging reduces variance. Practical rules applied here:\n\n- Per torsion, target band upper edge: f_hi = f_Nyq(Δt_truth). For Δt_truth = 10 fs, f_hi ≈ 50 THz — but alanine dipeptide has negligible real power above ~5 THz (libration shoulder); we therefore set the *signal* band of interest to ≤ 5 THz and treat [5 THz, 50 THz] as noise floor for SNR checks.\n- For the slowest mode of interest (smallest period relevant to dihedral *oscillation*, not transition): T_min ≈ 1 ps ⇒ f_min_osc = 1 THz.\n- Welch segment length must capture multiple periods of f_min_osc for resolution. A 32-ps Welch segment = 32 oscillation cycles of 1 ps → sufficient.\n- Number of segments ≥ 32 for the segment-mean to converge to 1 / √32 ≈ 18% estimator variance on PSD power, giving a usable 95% CI width on the *log-PSD band-power* metric.\n\n**Minimum ground-truth length therefore**: ≥ 32 segments × 32 ps = **~1 ns at the original fine cadence**, preferably ≥ 10 ns for alanine dipeptide, ≥ 100 ns for chignolin (slower dynamics need longer for MSM convergence).\n\n### 2.3 Storage and preprocessing\n- Every 1 fs (or every Δt_truth) frame retained for full fine-cadence analysis (memory: alanine dipeptide ≈ 24,000 atoms × 3 × 4 bytes × 10^7 frames ≈ 3 TB — *too large*). Store instead at 10 fs (= 10⁻¹⁴ s, which still Nyquist-folds to ≥100 THz, comfortably above any torsion signal). 10 ns at 10 fs stride = 10⁶ frames; alanine dipeptide storage ≈ 280 GB which is still excessive. Practical recommendation: store at 100 fs (still Nyquist-folds to 10 THz, encompassing the libration signal), 10⁶ frames = 100 ns = ~28 GB — tractable.\n- **Pre-declared**: report Δt_truth and f_Nyq(Δt_truth) explicitly; the chosen 100 fs cadence is sufficient only if the *signal band* is defined as ≤ 5 THz. The signal band definition must be locked before metric evaluation to avoid HARKing.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Stride sweep\n\n### 3.1 Sweep design\n| Cell | System | Stride S | Generated length (frames) | Effective Δt_gen |\n|------|--------|----------|---------------------------|-------------------|\n| 1 | alanine dipeptide | 1 ns | match MDGEN's max N_frames | ≈ 5 ps (if N=200) |\n| 2 | alanine dipeptide | 10 ns | same | same |\n| 3 | alanine dipeptide | 100 ns | same | same |\n| 4 | chignolin | 1 ns | same | same |\n| 5 | chignolin | 10 ns | same | same |\n| 6 | chignolin | 100 ns | same | same |\n\nWithin each cell, **N_gen = 32 independent generations** with distinct MDGEN noise seeds.\n\n### 3.2 H1 / H0 per band per cell\n\nFor a given (system, S, torsion):\n\n| Band | Boundary | H1 claim | H0 baseline |\n|------|----------|----------|-------------|\n| Preserved  | f ≤ f_Nyq(S) | Generated tracks ground truth within 95% CI (sanity check; expected true for any plausible model). | Generated ≠ ground truth |\n| **Lost**    | f_Nyq(S) < f ≤ f_Nyq(Δt_truth) | Generated power *within ground-truth 95% CI*. | Generated power ≪ ground-truth, indistinguishable from constant / linear interpolation. |\n| Noise floor | f > f_Nyq(Δt_truth) | Power at numerical noise floor. | n/a — uninterpretable. |\n\nPre-registered successes are decided only in the \"Lost\" band; the \"Preserved\" band is sanity, \"Noise floor\" is proof-of-capability.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Metric battery (priority-ordered)\n\n### 4.1 Primary — Welch PSD integrated power in lost band\nFor each torsion, compute Welch PSD of length-T windowed ground truth (segments averaged to give 95% CI), and Welch PSD of generated torsion time-series aligned at the same cadence. Define:\n> metric₁(X) = ∫_{f_Nyq(S)}^{f_Nyq(Δt_truth)} |PSD_gen(f)| / |PSD_gt(f)| df,\nestimated on a fixed log-spaced frequency grid to give equal per-decade weighting.\n\n**Reporting**: metric₁ as a scalar with bootstrap 95% CI over the n=32 generations.\n\n### 4.2 Secondary — Autocorrelation time τ_c\nCompute ACF up to half-length of trajectory; exponential fit to extract decay constant τ_c for each torsion. Report relative error |τ_c^gen − τ_c^gt| / τ_c^gt.\n\n### 4.3 Secondary — Torsion marginal distributions via JSD\n1D F(φ), F(ψ): bin into 36 bins of width 10° on S¹; histogram from generated vs. ground truth; Jensen–Shannon divergence in nats. 2D Ramachandran: 36×36 grid; same metric.\n\n### 4.4 Secondary — MSM/HMM implied timescales\nDiscretize ϕ / ψ into k = 4–8 microstates (k chosen by gap-heuristic on ground-truth spectral decomposition); fit MSM at lag times τ ∈ {0.1, 1, 10, 100} ps; report the slowest implied timescale t₂ and its uncertainty. To avoid leakage, the *microstate assignment* must come from ground truth only; the same assignment is then applied to generated frames.\n\n### 4.5 Tertiary — Hilbert amplitude & period\nFor each oscillation cycle (identified by zero-crossings), report median amplitude (envelope) and median period. This is sensitive to *symmetric* signal recovery where PSD power might look right but oscillation shape is off (e.g., rectified).\n\n### 4.6 Sanity — Cartesian and torsion RMSD\nPer-frame RMSD in cartesian coordinates after Kabsch alignment (sanity; expected to be small for MDGEN because it operates in cartesian space, *not* informative for the oscillation question). Torsion displacement RMSE (δφ, δψ, δχ), absolute and circular — likewise expected to be small for MDGEN because it preserves slow-motion agreement.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Controls\n\n| Name | Definition | Recoverable *Preserved* band | Recoverable *Lost* band | Role |\n|------|------------|------------------------------|-------------------------|------|\n| **L0** constant-copy | Generated(torch) = conditioning frame throughout | No (zero ACF) | No | Floor: bare minimum |\n| **L1** linear interpolation in atoms | Generated(i) = (1 − i/(N−1))·frame_0 + (i/(N−1))·frame_{N−1} (Kabsch-aligned each step) | Partially (low-pass) | No (torsion ≈ const or slow-drift, no oscillation) | **Non-negotiable beating baseline** for H1 |\n| **L2** linear interpolation in torsion | Same arithmetic in (φ, ψ, χ) space, then back to atoms where needed | Partially | No (sawtooth, not sinusoidal) | Forces comparison vs. the simplest torsion-space interpolant |\n| **L3** exact subsample passthrough | Generated = the input subsampled frames interpolated | Trivially yes | No (input had no lost-band content) | Upper bound on how well no-model can do |\n\n**Ablation interpretation logic (pre-registered):**\n- L0 and L1 are *required* to fall below H1 by a clear margin in the lost band. If L1 happens to coincide with MDGEN there, MDGEN is not recovering the oscillations — it is *not oscillating at all* in the relevant band, which is itself a publishable negative.\n- L2 provides a sanity check: MDGEN ≤ L2 means MDGEN is doing worse than a plain straight line in torsion space, which would be a serious finding.\n- L3 sets an upper bound on *passive* performance; it is mathematically known to score zero in the lost band because the lost band has zero ground-truth power at the subsampled frames.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Statistical design\n\n- **Sample size**: n = 32 independent generation seeds per (system, stride, torsion) cell, pre-declared. Total generations: 6 cells × 32 = 192. Plus 32 × 4 controls = 128 reference generations.\n- **Independent seeds**: MDGEN diffusion noise resampled from scratch per seed; conditioning frames identical across seeds within a cell.\n- **Pre-declared CIs**: 95% confidence intervals on metric₁ and τ_c via BCa bootstrap over the n = 32 seeds. Report median and 95% CI; do not report single-seed point estimates.\n- **Multiple-comparison control**: Bonferroni–Holm correction across torsions (ϕ, ψ, χ per residue) within a system. Pre-declared effect-size threshold for \"recovery\" must clear the corrected α.\n- **Reproducibility pre-registration**: code version + dataset checksum + seeds + division among band-of-interest locked before any aggregation step.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Pre-registered decision rules\n\nA run is *positive for H1* (recovers fast-timestep torsion dynamics) iff **all** of the following hold for each (system, S, torsion, seed sample) tested under the **strict primary reading**:\n\n1. **Primary metric in band**: 95% CI on metric₁ ⊆ [0.5, 2.0] (i.e., generated band power within a factor of 2 of ground truth, with CI tight enough to exclude H0).\n2. **Beats L1 by margin**: 95% CI on metric₁ strictly above L1's 95% CI, with non-overlap (mandatory; without this the experiment reports \"MDGEN does not oscillate,\" a valid negative answer).\n3. **Beats L2** (i.e., not worse than straight-line torsion interpolation): same CI non-overlap rule.\n4. **Preserved-band sanity**: 95% CI on metric₁ in preserved band within [0.7, 1.4] — confirms the model is not just hallucinating broadband noise.\n5. **ACF consistency |τ_c^gen − τ_c^gt| / τ_c^gt ≤ 0.3** in the preserved band.\n\nAny violation flips the per-(system, S, torsion) result to *negative*. The overall verdict is the modal sign across the 6 cells, separately reported per system.\n\n**Fallback A rules**: pre-stated bands collapse; rule 1 becomes \"trend agreement at stride resolution (lowest three periods only).\" **Fallback B rules**: reduce to \"Ramachandran JSD ≤ 0.05 nats\" with a stricter sample-size justification.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Failure modes and mitigations\n\n| Failure mode | Likelihood | Detection | Mitigation |\n|--------------|------------|-----------|------------|\n| MDGEN emits at Δt_gen ≫ 1 ps (i.e., cannot represent libration) | **High** — design parameter; expected for bridged-segment generation | Inspect Δt_gen between successive generated frames; compute f_Nyq(Δt_gen). If f_Nyq(Δt_gen) ≤ f_Nyq(S), then lost-band is empty → switch to **Fallback A**. | Report the cap; document under which S the test is only a \"trend-preservation\" test. |\n| Topology mismatch between generated and ground-truth structures (atoms renamed, ordering off, missing hydrogens) | Low–medium | `mdtraj.Topology` equality check before dihedral extraction; if mismatch, abort that cell and report as a separate failure. | Re-extract on a curated subset of residues where the residue index map is unambiguous. |\n| Insufficient ground-truth length for Welch convergence | Medium | Pre-flight: estimate segment count at planned length; if < 32 segments, run more ground truth. | Default to 10–100 ns minimum (§2.2); cap reporting bandwidth to what length supports. |\n| Torsion segmentation ambiguity deep in chignolin | Medium | Visual inspection of ψ_i time-series around suspected transitions; ambiguous residues reported as `masked`. | Pre-declared mask of well-defined vs. ambiguous residues; ambiguous ones excluded from metric₁ and reported in a separate table. |\n| Diffusion noise dominates generated time-series (washed out) | Medium | Visual: PSD of generated should not be flat-white-noise-like across the lost band. | Drop those seeds *only* if the diffusion is verifiably broken (e.g., cartesian RMSD to conditioning frame > some threshold); *do not* drop seeds where PSD is low but structured — that is a valid \"no recovery\" outcome. |\n| Insufficient statistical power (n < 32) | Medium (compute-dependent) | Pre-declared bootstrap CI width on metric₁; if CI width > factor-of-2, declare underpowered. | Reduce number of systems (drop chignolin) before reducing n. |\n| MDGEN did not converge during generation (rmsd_wrt to start/explodes) | Medium | Std of generated structures > 3× the std of the subsampled conditioning. | Discard seed; replace it; cap seed-replacement fraction. |\n| JSD numerics from sparse histogram bins | Low | If any bin has < 5 samples in either distribution, exclude it. | Pre-declare \"minimum-bins-used\" for each metric. |\n| Static (time-flat) torsion distribution recovered but no oscillation | **Expected outcome** | Direct visual: torsions oscillate over ps — or they don't. | This is a textbook Fallback-B-only positive: marginals are right, dynamics are not. |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Feasibility statement\n\nThis plan targets **out-of-sandbox execution** in a harness with:\n\n1. MDGEN `diffusion` module + checkpoint weights for the bridging-frame variant (as released by Microsoft Research), loadable with their published inference script.\n2. Python 3.x environment with `mdtraj`, `numpy`, `scipy`, `matplotlib`, and any MSM/HMM tooling (PyEMMA or deeptime).\n3. A GPU or sufficient CPU budget for 192 MDGEN generations + 128 control generations.\n4. Network access off — all dataset code paths must be local.\n\n**This `/workspace` cannot run it**: the environment notes (and confirmed inspection of `/workspace`) show no MDGEN repo, no preinstalled Python deps, network disabled, GPU unavailable. The plan above is therefore produced at design level only; runtime numbers are *not* included and will not be invented.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Out of scope (explicitly)\n\n- Cartesian free-energy landscape comparison. MDGEN is trained in cartesian space and small torsion oscillations have negligible cartesian RMSD, so cartesian-FE agreement would *not* answer the question.\n- Comparison to other generative-MD baselines (e.g., Timewarp, equivariant flows). Out of scope of the question \"does MDGEN recover *fast torsion* dynamics\"; orthogonal experiments may be authored separately.\n- Assessment of MDGEN's marginal stability / structure recovery beyond the band-overlap metric.\n\n---\n\n## 11. One-paragraph summary\n\nThe experiment takes a fine-cadence ground-truth MD trajectory of alanine dipeptide (Δt_truth ≈ 100 fs, length ≥ 10 ns) and chignolin (in-distribution), constructs subsampled conditioning trajectories at strides S ∈ {1, 10, 100} ns, generates bridging frames with MDGEN (n = 32 noise seeds per cell), and compares the resulting torsion time-series on ϕ, ψ (and χ for chignolin) to the ground truth by (i) Welch PSD integrated power in the *lost band* between the two Nyquist limits, (ii) autocorrelation time τ_c, (iii) Ramachandran JSD, (iv) MSM implied timescales over a lag-time sweep, plus Hilbert amplitude / period. Four pre-registered controls —